    gtag('js', new Date());
    gtag('config', 'G-TZ8921LR0K');
  </script>
  <script defer src="/analytics.js"></script>
</head>
<body data-page-type="blog_index">
  {% include "_site_header.html.j2" %}
  <div class="page">

    <section class="hero">
      <h1>AI Agent Trust Insights</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">Data-backed guides and comparisons for choosing open-source AI agents, coding agents, frameworks, guardrails, memory systems, and agent infrastructure.</p>
    </section>

    <div class="section-title">Research</div>
    <div class="post-grid">
      <article class="post-card featured">
        <div>
          <h2><a href="/blog/github-stars-dont-predict-ai-agent-trust/">GitHub Stars Don't Predict AI Agent Trust. I Scored 192 to Prove It.</a></h2>
          <div class="post-meta">May 31, 2026 &middot; 6 min read</div>
          <p class="post-excerpt">24 of the 30 most-starred AI agents ship with no build provenance. Here's the full list, the six that get it right, and why stars are the wrong way to choose an agent.</p>
          <a href="/blog/github-stars-dont-predict-ai-agent-trust/" class="read-more">Read article &rarr;</a>
        </div>
        <div class="metric-strip">
          <div class="metric"><strong>{{ total }}</strong><span>agents tracked</span></div>
          <div class="metric"><strong>{{ categories|length }}</strong><span>categories</span></div>
          <div class="metric"><strong>{{ top_agent.trust_score }}</strong><span>top HVTrust</span></div>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="section-title">Trend Reports</div>
    <div class="post-grid">
      <article class="post-card">
        <h2><a href="/blog/mcp-server-launch/">HVTracker Is Now an MCP Server: Trust Checks Before Your Agent Connects</a></h2>
        <div class="post-meta">June 21, 2026 &middot; 5 min read</div>
        <p class="post-excerpt">Our public trust registry is now an MCP server. Point any MCP client at hvtracker.net/mcp and your agent can verify any server, package, or agent before it connects — three read-only tools, no auth, open verdict.</p>
        <a href="/blog/mcp-server-launch/" class="read-more">Read article &rarr;</a>
      </article>
      <article class="post-card">
        <h2><a href="/blog/state-of-ai-agent-supply-chain-trust-2026/">The State of AI Agent Supply-Chain Trust (2026): 272 Agents, Graded</a></h2>
        <div class="post-meta">June 21, 2026 &middot; 8 min read</div>
        <p class="post-excerpt">We graded 272 open-source AI agents on supply-chain trust. Only 13% earn an A; 43% land at D. 17% publish provenance, the median OSSF Scorecard is 5.3/10. The full state of the ecosystem.</p>
        <a href="/blog/state-of-ai-agent-supply-chain-trust-2026/" class="read-more">Read article &rarr;</a>
      </article>
      <article class="post-card">
        <h2><a href="/blog/ai-agents-mcp-servers-trust/">Hundreds of AI Agents Now Ship MCP Servers. How Many Can You Actually Trust?</a></h2>
        <div class="post-meta">June 21, 2026 &middot; 6 min read</div>
        <p class="post-excerpt">45% of the agents we track now implement or declare an MCP server — and 76% of them ship no build provenance. As MCP tool-poisoning becomes the top 2026 attack, here's who you can verify.</p>
        <a href="/blog/ai-agents-mcp-servers-trust/" class="read-more">Read article &rarr;</a>
      </article>
      <article class="post-card">
        <h2><a href="/blog/trapdoor-supply-chain-provenance/">TrapDoor Hit npm, PyPI, and Crates at Once. Provenance Is the Signal That Catches It.</a></h2>
        <div class="post-meta">June 21, 2026 &middot; 6 min read</div>
        <p class="post-excerpt">The 2026 TrapDoor campaign weaponized three registries at once and planted hidden instructions in CLAUDE.md. Build provenance detects exactly this — and 83% of AI agents don't publish it.</p>
        <a href="/blog/trapdoor-supply-chain-provenance/" class="read-more">Read article &rarr;</a>
      </article>
      <article class="post-card">
        <h2><a href="/blog/runtime-trust-is-live/">Runtime Trust Is Live on HVTracker</a></h2>
        <div class="post-meta">June 5, 2026 &middot; 5 min read</div>
        <p class="post-excerpt">HVTracker v3.2 adds public runtime-trust discovery and an experimental score lab. See how the current top 10 would move under the first calibration.</p>
        <a href="/blog/runtime-trust-is-live/" class="read-more">Read article &rarr;</a>
      </article>
      <article class="post-card">
        <h2><a href="/blog/you-are-not-installing-what-you-think/">You're Not Installing What You Think You Are</a></h2>
        <div class="post-meta">June 2, 2026 &middot; 6 min read</div>
        <p class="post-excerpt">122 million weekly downloads, zero proof the package matches the source code. 83% of AI agents ship without build provenance. Here's what sits in the gap.</p>
        <a href="/blog/you-are-not-installing-what-you-think/" class="read-more">Read article &rarr;</a>
      </article>
      <article class="post-card">
        <h2><a href="/blog/how-to-evaluate-ai-agent-safety/">How to Evaluate AI Agent Safety: 5 Signals That Actually Matter</a></h2>
        <div class="post-meta">May 27, 2026 &middot; 6 min read</div>
        <p class="post-excerpt">GitHub stars measure popularity, not trustworthiness. Here are the evidence-based signals that actually help evaluate whether an open-source AI agent is safe to adopt.</p>
        <a href="/blog/how-to-evaluate-ai-agent-safety/" class="read-more">Read article &rarr;</a>
      </article>
      <article class="post-card">
        <h2><a href="/blog/most-starred-ai-agents-no-provenance/">The Most Popular AI Agents Ship Without Provenance</a></h2>
        <div class="post-meta">May 30, 2026 &middot; 5 min read</div>
        <p class="post-excerpt">375k stars, 184k stars, 167k stars — and zero build provenance. We checked the 10 most-starred AI agents. Eight ship without any package attestation.</p>
        <a href="/blog/most-starred-ai-agents-no-provenance/" class="read-more">Read article &rarr;</a>
      </article>
      <article class="post-card">
        <h2><a href="/blog/codex-vs-claude-code/">Codex vs Claude Code: Which Coding Agent Is Easier to Trust?</a></h2>
        <div class="post-meta">June 1, 2026 &middot; 5 min read</div>
        <p class="post-excerpt">Claude Code has more stars. Codex ranks dramatically higher on HVTracker. The gap comes from provenance, signed commits, and public verifiability.</p>
        <a href="/blog/codex-vs-claude-code/" class="read-more">Read article &rarr;</a>
      </article>
      <article class="post-card">
        <h2><a href="/blog/coding-agents-trust-rankings/">Coding Agents Ranked by Trust, Not Stars — The Results Are Embarrassing</a></h2>
        <div class="post-meta">May 30, 2026 &middot; 6 min read</div>
        <p class="post-excerpt">opencode (167k stars) ranks #127. GPT Pilot has 1% signed commits. Only one coding agent cracks the global top 10.</p>
        <a href="/blog/coding-agents-trust-rankings/" class="read-more">Read article &rarr;</a>
      </article>
      <article class="post-card">
        <h2><a href="/blog/ai-agent-frameworks-ranked-by-trust/">LangChain vs LangGraph vs CrewAI vs AutoGPT — Ranked by Trust, Not Hype</a></h2>
        <div class="post-meta">May 30, 2026 &middot; 7 min read</div>
        <p class="post-excerpt">LangGraph #1, AutoGPT #39, LlamaIndex #126, smolagents #138. Stars don't tell this story — provenance, scorecards, and signed commits do.</p>
        <a href="/blog/ai-agent-frameworks-ranked-by-trust/" class="read-more">Read article &rarr;</a>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="section-title">Top Category Comparisons</div>
    <div class="post-grid">
      {% for article in articles %}
      <article class="post-card">
        <h2><a href="/blog/{{ article.slug }}/">{{ article.title }}</a></h2>
        <div class="post-meta">{{ article.date }} &middot; {{ article.read_time }} min read &middot; {{ article.category }}</div>
        <p class="post-excerpt">{{ article.excerpt }}</p>
        <a href="/blog/{{ article.slug }}/" class="read-more">Read comparison &rarr;</a>
      </article>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>

    <footer>
      <a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a>
